<BaseLayout
  title="Threat Model"
  description="Explicit BeaconGuard threat model covering release lifecycle, dual authorization, AI provider and enterprise boundaries, evidence, and verification threats."
>
  <section class="stack-section">
    <h1>Threat Model</h1>

    <p>
      This document describes the <strong>explicit threat model</strong> for
      BeaconGuard. It enumerates assumptions, assets, and threat categories
      relevant to release lifecycle, Request Admission Authorization,
      Result-to-Action Authorization, evidence, and BeaconGuard Verify.
    </p>

    <p>
      The goal is not to claim absolute security, but to make <strong>attack surfaces
      and failure modes explicit and reviewable</strong>. Mitigations listed here are
      product design intents already reflected in the architecture; they are not
      guarantees against every deployment-specific failure.
    </p>

    <p>See also: <a href="/docs/dfd-threat-flow">DFD and Threat Flow</a>.</p>

    <h2>Security Assumptions</h2>

    <p>BeaconGuard operates under assumptions including:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Runtime authorization executes in a controlled customer environment</li>
      <li>Cryptographic primitives are correctly implemented and not broken</li>
      <li>Compliance Playbooks are authored and Signed Immutable Release Artifacts are signed by parties trusted by the customer</li>
      <li>Identity providers, network controls, secrets, and host hardening are operated by the customer</li>
      <li>Transport-level security (for example TLS) is provided by the deployment environment</li>
      <li>Evidence-provider configuration and operational monitoring are customer responsibilities when durability or append-only behavior is required</li>
      <li>Applications honor ALLOW/DENY and do not bypass BeaconGuard on outbound or return paths</li>
    </ul>

    <p>If these assumptions do not hold, BeaconGuard control assertions may not apply.</p>

    <h2>Protected Assets</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Compliance Playbooks and compiled control representations</li>
      <li>Signed Immutable Release Artifacts and Active Verified Release integrity</li>
      <li>Authorization decision semantics (ALLOW / DENY only)</li>
      <li>Verifiable Decision Records and reconstructable decision context</li>
      <li>Request–result–action correlation identifiers for investigation</li>
      <li>Investigation replay, reconstruction, and verifiable export integrity</li>
      <li>Provenance via release_identity and playbook_identity</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Threat Categories</h2>

    <h3>Release Tampering or Unauthorized Activation</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Threat:</strong> An attacker modifies playbook content, substitutes a
      release, or activates an unverified artifact so runtime evaluation uses
      unapproved controls.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Relevant controls / design intent:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Signing of release packages before distribution</li>
      <li>Release Verification and Activation before an Active Verified Release governs evaluation</li>
      <li>Recording of release_identity on Verifiable Decision Records</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>System Connector Compromise</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Threat:</strong> A compromised System Connector injects false context,
      strips required trust signals, or presents malformed normalized inputs.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Relevant controls / design intent:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Connector does not evaluate controls or activate releases</li>
      <li>Runtime still performs Request Validation and Context Normalization plus Trust, Freshness, and Replay Checks</li>
      <li>Fail-closed DENY when required signals are missing or invalid</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Customer hardening of connector hosts and credentials remains in scope for
      the deployment environment.
    </p>

    <h3>Control-Plane Compromise</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Threat:</strong> Compromise of the BeaconGuard Control Plane /
      management plane allows unauthorized playbook changes, signing, or
      distribution.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Relevant controls / design intent:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Separation of Govern from Authorize — Control Plane is outside the per-request path</li>
      <li>Runtime accepts only verified, activated releases</li>
      <li>Explicit activation rather than silent draft evaluation</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Key management, access control, and operational monitoring of the control
      plane are shared deployment responsibilities.
    </p>

    <h3>Identity Provider (IdP) Compromise or Misbinding</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Threat:</strong> Compromised or misconfigured identity signals cause
      incorrect role or actor attributes to reach evaluation.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Relevant controls / design intent:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>BeaconGuard evaluates attributes as provided in trusted request context; the customer IdP does not make the BeaconGuard decision</li>
      <li>Missing or invalid required attributes fail closed as DENY</li>
    </ul>
    <p>IdP integrity and attribute binding remain customer-owned.</p>

    <h3>Replay-State Provider Failure or Manipulation</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Threat:</strong> When a replay-state provider is used, an attacker
      suppresses, forges, or desynchronizes replay state to force incorrect
      admission outcomes.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Relevant controls / design intent:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Replay-state providers, when used, are external to core decision evaluation and must be configured for fail-closed operation</li>
      <li>Failed freshness or replay checks return DENY</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Provider availability and integrity are deployment-dependent.</p>

    <h3>Evidence Suppression or Alteration</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Threat:</strong> An attacker deletes, alters, or suppresses Verifiable
      Decision Records to hide DENY/ALLOW history.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Relevant controls / design intent:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Integrity-protected decision evidence designed for investigation replay and verification</li>
      <li>Support for append-only retention when configured with a required evidence provider</li>
      <li>release_identity and decision context embedded in records</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Universal append-only or immutable-store guarantees are not claimed without
      the configured evidence provider and customer operational controls.
    </p>

    <h3>Signing Key Compromise</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Threat:</strong> Compromise of release-signing or related keys allows
      forged Signed Immutable Release Artifacts.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Relevant controls / design intent:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Runtime verification of release signatures before activation</li>
      <li>Explicit rollback and re-activation processes after key events</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Key custody, rotation, and HSM or KMS choices are customer / deployment
      responsibilities.
    </p>

    <h3>Unauthorized Authorization Bypass</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Threat:</strong> An attacker attempts to force ALLOW through malformed
      inputs, reused prior authority, or permissive fallbacks.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Relevant controls / design intent:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Fail-closed semantics</li>
      <li>Explicit input normalization</li>
      <li>No review-as-permission path</li>
      <li>No reuse of prior authority as a substitute for new governed authorization</li>
      <li>Deterministic evaluation with ALLOW / DENY only</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Bypass of Request Admission or Return-Path Controls</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Threat:</strong> Applications invoke the AI provider without required
      request authorization, or apply a provider result / execute an enterprise
      action without required Result-to-Action Authorization.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Relevant controls / design intent:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Product placement assumes BeaconGuard is on both the outbound provider path and the return/action path where enforcement is configured</li>
      <li>Network and application architecture must prevent silent bypass</li>
      <li>Provider success must not be treated as BeaconGuard authorization</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Bypass prevention outside BeaconGuard’s process boundary is a customer
      deployment responsibility.
    </p>

    <h3>Treating AI Output as Authority</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Threat:</strong> An application or operator treats an AI-generated
      result, tool proposal, report, or AI-assisted insight as sufficient
      authorization for enterprise effect.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Relevant controls / design intent:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>AI-generated results are untrusted proposals</li>
      <li>Declared intended enterprise actions require independent authorization where configured</li>
      <li>AI-Assisted Insights are non-authoritative and outside the trusted path</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Verification and Investigation Threats</h2>

    <h3>Missing Source Evidence</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Threat:</strong> Required source artifacts or fields are absent, so
      reconstruction cannot complete honestly.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Relevant controls / design intent:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Missing fields reported as unavailable</li>
      <li>No fabrication of missing artifacts or model reasoning</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Inconsistent Evidence</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Threat:</strong> Related decision records, hashes, or bindings disagree
      across a governed transaction.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Relevant controls / design intent:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Integrity checks on evidence and export packages where configured</li>
      <li>Investigation surfaces must surface inconsistency rather than silently reconcile</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Reconstruction from Noncanonical Data</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Threat:</strong> Reviewers reconstruct decisions from untrusted browser
      state, demo fixtures, or noncanonical copies.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Relevant controls / design intent:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Canonical evidence and server-side reconstruction authority</li>
      <li>Browser-side claims are untrusted for integrity conclusions</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Cross-Tenant Reconstruction</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Threat:</strong> Investigation access crosses tenant boundaries
      incorrectly.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Relevant controls / design intent:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Tenant isolation controls (lab-proven; customer-live residual risk remains)</li>
      <li>Access control and tenant binding for Verify surfaces</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Report Provenance Loss</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Threat:</strong> Executive or investigation reports lose linkage to
      underlying decision evidence.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Relevant controls / design intent:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Report provenance to Verifiable Decision Records</li>
      <li>Reports do not grant runtime authority</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Export Tampering</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Threat:</strong> An attacker alters a verifiable export package after
      generation.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Relevant controls / design intent:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Integrity checks on export packages where configured</li>
      <li>Export signing and verification remain server-side responsibilities; keys are not browser-held</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>AI-Generated Explanation Used as Authority</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Threat:</strong> An AI-assisted insight or generated explanation is
      treated as authoritative reconstruction or as runtime permission.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Relevant controls / design intent:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>AI-Assisted Insights labeled non-authoritative</li>
      <li>Insights remain outside trusted reconstruction and authorization paths</li>
      <li>Verify cannot grant runtime authority</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Non-Goals and Explicit Exclusions</h2>

    <p>BeaconGuard does <strong>not</strong> claim to mitigate:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Compromise of the underlying operating system or hypervisor</li>
      <li>Insider threats with legitimate signing authority</li>
      <li>Vulnerabilities in AI model training data or model internals</li>
      <li>Misuse after an authorized action, outside the declared action contract, or outside the BeaconGuard-controlled path</li>
      <li>Replacement of SIEM, IAM, GRC, or customer security programs</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      BeaconGuard governs whether a returned AI result is authorized for the
      declared enterprise action. Misuse after an authorized action, outside the
      declared action contract, or outside the BeaconGuard-controlled path remains
      a customer responsibility.
    </p>

    <h2>Residual Risk</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Misconfiguration can DENY legitimate governed execution</li>
      <li>Playbook authoring errors can encode incorrect intent</li>
      <li>Key management failures undermine release integrity assertions</li>
      <li>Evidence durability depends on provider configuration and operations</li>
      <li>Application bypass outside BeaconGuard’s process boundary remains customer-owned</li>
    </ul>

    <p>BeaconGuard prioritizes <strong>visibility and determinism</strong> over silent tolerance.</p>

